module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.EffectiveCartier.Intersection
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.EffectiveCartierEtaleEquiv
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ZeroSchemeLocallyPrincipal
import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# The zero scheme of a section of an invertible module is an effective Cartier divisor exactly
# when the section is regular

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let `L` be an
invertible `𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a global section of `L`.  This file proves part (4) of
Stacks 0846 for an invertible `L` and nothing more: the zero scheme `Z(s)` of Stacks 0845 is an
effective Cartier divisor on `X` if and only if `s` is a regular section of `L` in the sense of
Stacks 0843.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_iff_isRegularSection` is part (4)
  of Stacks 0846, at the source's hypotheses.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isRegularSection_of_isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_of_isRegularSection` are its two
  halves.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_affine_chart_unitIso_of_isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_one`: a sheaf
  of modules of rank one on the small étale site is trivial on an affine étale chart through
  every point of `X`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_isRegular_span_definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_of_unitIso`: on such
  a chart the zero scheme of a regular section is cut out by a single nonzerodivisor.

## Implementation notes

No chart data is assumed anywhere: invertibility of `L` is the only hypothesis on `L`, and the
chart data that the chartwise statements of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeAssertions.lean` and
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeProperties.lean` carry — a generator `t` of
`L(U)` with a coordinate functional `l₀` — is *supplied* here chart by chart, which is the same
move that
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_isInvertible`
makes for the realization of the evaluation ideal by a closed subspace.  An invertible module on
the small étale site of an algebraic space is finite locally free of rank one (Stacks 0B8Q,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_one_of_isInvertible`), so on a covering family
of every chart it is isomorphic to the structure sheaf, and the trivialization gives the pair
`t`, `l₀` as the two directions of the induced linear equivalence on sections.

Both sides of the equivalence are local, and the two localities are the two halves.

*Regularity is local because monicity of a morphism of sheaves of modules is injectivity on
sections over every object, and injectivity on sections descends along a covering family*
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.module_injective_app_of_covering_family`, by separatedness of the
source sheaf).  So for the half from effective Cartier-ness to regularity it suffices to have
injectivity of multiplication by `s` over the members of one covering family of each chart.  A
chart in such a family is chosen below a chart trivializing `L`, below a chart trivializing
`𝓘_{Z(s)}` — which is finite locally free of rank one precisely because `Z(s)` is an effective
Cartier divisor, Stacks 0B8Q again — and affine.  There the defining ideal of `Z(s)` is both the
principal ideal generated by the coordinate `l₀(s)` of the section
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_eq` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_eq_span_singleton`) and the principal ideal
generated by a nonzerodivisor
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_isRegular_span_definingIdealAt_of_unitIso`), and two generators
of one principal ideal differ by a unit, so `l₀(s)` is a nonzerodivisor too.

*Effective Cartier-ness is local because of Stacks 083C*: a closed subspace all of whose points
have an affine étale neighbourhood on which it is cut out by a nonzerodivisor is an effective
Cartier divisor, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_hasEffectiveCartierEtalePullbacks` fed
through `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.hasEffectiveCartierEtalePullbacks_of_pointwise_charts` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isSchemeEffectiveCartierPullback_of_isRegular_span`.  So for the half
from regularity it suffices to produce, through every point of `X`, an affine étale chart on
which `Z(s)` is cut out by a nonzerodivisor; rank one of `L` produces an affine chart through
every point on which `L` is trivial, and there the nonzerodivisor is `l₀(s)`.

